CAT = "cisaOperations"; SEG = "OPS"
Q = [
("What should come first when setting up IT asset management?",
 "A complete asset inventory", ["A plan to replace every laptop", "A new helpdesk ticket category", "A discount deal with one vendor"],
 "Assets that are not known cannot be protected, patched, licensed or retired properly. The inventory, with an owner for each asset, is the base for everything else."),
("Old servers are sent to a recycler. What evidence should an IS auditor expect to see?",
 "Certificates of data destruction", ["A signed asset transfer form only", "The recycler's general privacy policy", "An email saying the servers were sent"],
 "The organization needs proof that storage media were wiped or destroyed, matched to the asset inventory, so no data leaves on retired equipment."),
("Operators can change job parameters in the batch scheduler without approval. What should the IS auditor review?",
 "The scheduler's change logs", ["The operators' leave records", "The data center floor plan", "The printer supply orders"],
 "Unapproved changes to job schedules or parameters can run the wrong programs or data. Logs of scheduler changes, matched to approved requests, show whether this happens."),
("An operator restarts a failed overnight job by overriding a control step. Which control matters most?",
 "Logging and review of the override", ["Rerunning the job twice to be sure", "Removing the step from the job", "Emailing users after the restart"],
 "Overrides are sometimes needed, but each one should be recorded and reviewed by someone independent, so misuse or errors are noticed."),
("Orders pass from a web shop to the warehouse system through an interface. Which control best shows that none were lost?",
 "Reconciling counts on both sides", ["Reviewing the interface specification", "Checking the interface's uptime report", "Reading the interface design notes"],
 "Comparing counts and totals sent with those received confirms complete transfer. Rejected records should land in an error queue that is worked and cleared."),
("Records rejected by an interface go to an error queue. What is the main control concern?",
 "The queue is monitored and cleared", ["The queue runs on a supported database", "The queue is backed up every night", "The queue keeps items for a full year"],
 "An unmonitored error queue means transactions silently drop out of processing. Someone should own the queue, fix and resubmit items, and track their age."),
("A spreadsheet maintained by one analyst calculates figures used in the financial statements. What is the main risk?",
 "Errors go undetected without controls", ["The file is saved on a network drive", "Other staff cannot open the file", "The spreadsheet cannot be printed in full"],
 "End-user computing tools often lack change control, testing and access protection. Critical spreadsheets should be inventoried, protected and independently checked."),
("Finance teams use several unapproved cloud tools. What should IT do first?",
 "Find the tools and assess their risk", ["Block every cloud tool for finance users", "Let finance keep using them as they are", "Ask the vendors to sign new contracts"],
 "The first step is visibility: find what is in use and what data it holds, then approve, replace or retire each tool under normal governance."),
("Which activity best helps IT plan for future capacity?",
 "Trend analysis of utilization data", ["Buying the largest server available", "Waiting for users to report slowness", "Reducing the number of backups"],
 "Monitoring use over time and projecting it forward shows when processing, storage or network limits will be reached, so upgrades can be planned before service suffers."),
("A database server has no redundant power supply or network link. Which concern does this raise?",
 "A single point of failure", ["Excessive licensing costs", "Weak password complexity", "Too many audit log entries"],
 "One failed component would take the service down. Availability management looks for such points and adds redundancy where the business impact warrants it."),
("Incident tickets are often closed with no cause or resolution code. What is the main concern?",
 "Recurring causes cannot be analyzed", ["Tickets will take longer to close", "Users will log fewer incidents", "The service desk will need more staff"],
 "Without cause and resolution data, problem management cannot spot trends or find the root cause of repeat incidents."),
("How should an IT service desk set the priority of an incident?",
 "By its impact and urgency", ["By the order tickets arrive", "By the caller's job title", "By the ticket's length"],
 "Priority combines how much of the business is affected with how quickly harm grows, so the most damaging incidents are handled first."),
("What is a known error record used for?",
 "A documented cause with a workaround", ["A list of staff who caused outages", "A record of failed changes and their owners", "A count of incidents for each month"],
 "When a problem's cause is found but not yet fixed, the known error record lets the service desk apply the workaround quickly when the incident recurs."),
("To test change management, from which population should an IS auditor select a sample?",
 "Changes found in the production system", ["Change tickets marked as approved", "Changes chosen by the change manager", "Changes made in the test system"],
 "Sampling from what actually changed in production, then tracing back to approvals, finds unauthorized changes. Starting from approved tickets would miss changes that never had one."),
("Developers have permanent write access to the production environment. What is the main risk?",
 "Unapproved changes reach production", ["Production will run more slowly overall", "Change tickets will take longer to close", "Test data will become out of date"],
 "Write access lets changes bypass testing and approval. Production access should go to operations staff, with any developer access temporary, logged and reviewed."),
("A vendor patch cannot be applied to a legacy control system. What should the organization do?",
 "Isolate it and add other controls", ["Accept the risk with no further action", "Apply the patch to production untested", "Replace the system within the week"],
 "If a fix is impossible, the risk is reduced in other ways, such as network segmentation, strict access and monitoring, and the residual risk is formally accepted."),
("What is the main value of an accurate configuration management database?",
 "It shows components and dependencies", ["It records each user's access rights", "It replaces the need for change approval", "It schedules all production batch jobs"],
 "The CMDB records configuration items and how they relate, so the impact of a change or failure can be judged before it happens."),
("Which control best protects system logs from being altered by the administrators they record?",
 "Forwarding logs to a separate server", ["Storing logs on each server's local disk", "Letting admins archive their own logs", "Keeping logs for a longer period"],
 "Sending logs in real time to a system administrators cannot change keeps them as reliable evidence of what those administrators did."),
("Who should review the activity logs of database administrators?",
 "Someone independent of the DBAs", ["The DBAs who generated them", "The application's business users", "The database software vendor"],
 "Privileged users could hide their own misuse if they reviewed their own logs. Review by security or another independent party gives assurance."),
("A table of orders holds customer numbers that do not exist in the customer table. Which database control would have prevented this?",
 "Referential integrity", ["Field-level encryption", "Index rebuilding", "Input masking"],
 "Referential integrity requires a foreign key to match an existing primary key, so orphan records cannot be created."),
("Two users update the same account record at the same moment and one change is lost. Which control prevents this?",
 "Record locking", ["Data mirroring", "Daily backups", "Audit logging"],
 "Concurrency controls such as locking make one update wait until the other completes, so neither overwrites the other."),
("A transaction updates databases in two cities. Which mechanism makes sure it is committed in both or neither?",
 "Two-phase commit", ["Round-robin scheduling", "Load balancing", "Data partitioning"],
 "In two-phase commit, each site first confirms it is ready, then all commit together. If any site cannot, all roll back, keeping the data consistent."),
("A long overnight batch job fails near its end and must be rerun from the very start, missing the morning deadline. Which control would have avoided the full rerun?",
 "Checkpoint and restart procedures", ["A larger printer for batch reports", "More frequent password changes", "A second copy of the job schedule"],
 "Checkpoints save the job's state at set points, so after a failure processing restarts from the last checkpoint rather than from the beginning."),
("A database is denormalized to speed up reports. Which risk increases?",
 "Inconsistent copies of a fact", ["Weaker access control on each table", "Slower backups of the whole database", "Loss of the database's index files"],
 "Storing the same fact in several places means an update can change one copy and miss another, so extra controls are needed to keep copies in step."),
("What is a data dictionary mainly used for?",
 "Defining each data element's meaning", ["Holding the database's backup schedule", "Storing user passwords securely", "Logging every query users run"],
 "A data dictionary records each element's name, definition, format and owner, so systems and users interpret data the same way."),
("A business impact analysis sets a maximum tolerable downtime of 24 hours for a process. What must be true of its recovery time objective?",
 "It must be shorter than 24 hours", ["It must be longer than 24 hours", "It must equal the recovery point objective", "It can be set without regard to the MTD"],
 "The maximum tolerable downtime is the longest outage the business can survive. Systems must be restored within the RTO with time left to verify and catch up on work before that limit is reached."),
("Many virtual machines run on one host, and some no one recognizes. What is the main concern?",
 "Unmanaged VMs that miss patching", ["The host needs a larger license", "Backups of the host take longer", "Users notice slower response times"],
 "VM sprawl leaves machines without owners, patches or monitoring, each one a weak point that also shares the host with critical systems."),
("Which control matters most for a hypervisor that hosts production servers?",
 "Hardening and tight admin access", ["Placing it in the same VLAN as users", "Running it on the newest hardware only", "Sharing its admin account among the team"],
 "A compromised hypervisor exposes every guest on it, so it needs a hardened configuration, prompt patching and tightly limited administrative access."),
("A provider's monthly report says uptime met the service level, but users report many outages. What should the IS auditor do?",
 "Compare it with internal outage data", ["Accept the provider's report as given", "Ask the provider to restate its figures", "Lower the service level target"],
 "The provider's figures may measure something different or be wrong. Independent data from the organization's own monitoring and tickets tests the claim."),
("A company has merged with another and moved several processes. Which continuity document most needs refreshing first?",
 "The business impact analysis", ["The asset disposal log", "The help desk call scripts", "The software license register"],
 "Recovery priorities and objectives come from the business impact analysis, so a major change to processes means redoing it before the plans that depend on it."),
("Who is best placed to judge how critical a business process is during a business impact analysis?",
 "The business process owners", ["The IT infrastructure manager", "The IS audit manager", "The software vendor"],
 "Business owners understand the financial, legal and customer effects of losing their processes. IT can say what is technically possible, not what the business needs."),
("A business needs a recovery point objective close to zero for its trading system. Which approach is needed?",
 "Synchronous replication offsite", ["A weekly full backup sent offsite", "A nightly incremental backup", "Monthly snapshots kept for a year"],
 "To lose almost no data, every write must reach the second site before it is confirmed. Periodic backups always lose the changes made since the last copy."),
("How does a shorter recovery time objective usually affect cost?",
 "Recovery costs rise", ["Recovery costs fall", "Costs stay the same", "Backups need less storage"],
 "Recovering faster needs more ready capacity, such as standby equipment and replicated data, so the cost of the recovery solution goes up."),
("What is most important to keep a business continuity plan effective over time?",
 "Regular testing and updating", ["Keeping it in the IT manager's office", "Limiting it to IT staff only", "Writing it in great detail once"],
 "Systems, people and suppliers change. Regular tests and reviews after changes keep the plan workable when it is needed."),
("A disaster recovery test has just ended. What is the most important output?",
 "Results compared with objectives", ["A list of staff who attended the test", "A list of the hardware used at the site", "A draft budget for the next test"],
 "Test results should be compared with recovery time and point objectives, gaps recorded and fixes assigned, so the next real recovery goes better."),
("Which item belongs in a business continuity plan's communications section?",
 "An up-to-date call tree", ["The full asset register", "The data retention schedule", "The help desk's ticket backlog"],
 "A current call tree and contact list let the organization reach staff, suppliers and customers quickly when normal channels are disrupted."),
("Under a grandfather-father-son scheme, what do the 'grandfather' backups usually represent?",
 "The oldest kept cycle, often monthly", ["The most recent nightly incremental copy", "Copies kept on the same server", "Copies made after each change"],
 "The scheme keeps daily (son), weekly (father) and monthly (grandfather) sets, so older restore points remain available while media are reused."),
("Backups are encrypted before going offsite. Which recovery risk must be planned for?",
 "Keys being unavailable at recovery", ["Tapes taking longer to write", "Backups no longer needing labels", "Users seeing slower restores at times"],
 "If the encryption keys are lost with the primary site, the backups cannot be read. Keys must be held securely and be available at the recovery site."),
("Which backup measure best protects against ransomware that also targets backups?",
 "Immutable or offline copies", ["More frequent full backups online", "Backups on the same file server", "Backups kept for a longer period"],
 "Ransomware often seeks out reachable backups. Copies that cannot be changed or are disconnected from the network survive the attack."),
("Two companies agree to host each other's systems after a disaster. What is the main weakness?",
 "Capacity may not be there", ["It always costs more than a hot site", "It cannot be put in writing", "It needs a third party to run it"],
 "Each partner's systems and workloads change over time, and in a regional disaster both may need help. Compatibility and spare capacity are hard to guarantee."),
("An organization contracts with a commercial hot site shared by many customers. What should the IS auditor check?",
 "How many clients could invoke it at once", ["Whether the site has its own security guards", "The number of the provider's sales staff", "The age of the site's buildings"],
 "If too many subscribers in one region declare a disaster together, the site may be oversubscribed. Contracts and provider data show how this is managed."),
("Which RAID level mirrors data so that each disk has an identical copy?",
 "RAID 1", ["RAID 0", "RAID 5", "RAID 6"],
 "RAID 1 writes the same data to two disks, so either can fail without data loss. RAID 0 stripes with no redundancy."),
("What does clustering two application servers mainly provide?",
 "Failover if one server stops", ["Protection against data corruption", "Lower software license counts", "A replacement for offsite backups"],
 "In a cluster, a surviving node takes over the workload, improving availability. Clusters do not protect against data corruption, so backups are still needed."),
("Which insurance covers income lost while operations are stopped after a disaster?",
 "Business interruption cover", ["Equipment breakdown cover", "Fidelity bond for employee theft", "Media and data reconstruction cover"],
 "Business interruption cover pays for lost income and extra expenses during the outage. Insurance supports continuity planning but does not restore service."),
("Who should own the business continuity program?",
 "Senior management", ["The IT help desk", "The IS auditor", "The facilities team"],
 "Continuity is a business risk, so senior management sets priorities, approves resources and is accountable for the program."),
("Only one engineer knows how to restore a critical system. What should the continuity plan address?",
 "Cross-training and documentation", ["A higher salary for the engineer", "An on-call rota for the engineer", "Removing the system from the plan"],
 "Key person dependency is a continuity risk. Documented procedures and trained backups mean recovery does not depend on one person being available."),
("A core application runs on a cloud platform. How should the organization gain assurance about its recovery?",
 "Review the provider's recovery test evidence", ["Assume the provider's SLA covers it all", "Ask the provider's account manager", "Rely on the uptime figure in the contract"],
 "Resilience is shared: the organization reviews independent assurance on the provider's recovery capability and still plans for its own data and configurations."),
("Where should copies of the disaster recovery plan be kept?",
 "Offsite and reachable in an outage", ["Only on the primary site's file server", "In the data center's server room", "With the recovery manager only"],
 "A plan stored only at the affected site may be lost with it. Copies offsite, or in a system that does not depend on the primary site, can be used in a disaster."),
("What should decide when a disaster is formally declared?",
 "Set criteria and named authority", ["The first manager who reaches the site", "A vote of the whole IT team", "The provider of the hot site"],
 "Clear thresholds and named people with authority to declare avoid delays and confusion about whether to invoke the recovery plan."),
("Why should key suppliers be included in business continuity planning?",
 "Recovery may rely on them", ["Suppliers must approve the plan", "It lowers the price of their services", "It moves the recovery cost onto suppliers"],
 "A process can only recover if the services and parts it relies on are also available, so supplier recovery arrangements and contacts belong in the plan."),
("Which test lets team members talk through their recovery roles step by step, without moving any systems?",
 "A structured walk-through", ["A full interruption test of the site", "A parallel test", "A live failover"],
 "In a walk-through the team meets and steps through the plan to find gaps and check roles. It is low cost and does not disturb operations."),
("What is the main risk if hardware is not maintained on the schedule the manufacturer recommends?",
 "More unplanned failures", ["Higher licensing costs", "Slower processing speed", "Shorter backup windows"],
 "Skipped preventive maintenance raises the chance of failures and can void support contracts, hurting availability."),
]
